A list of problems that a programming language should enable solving:


* We must be able to generate code that can also be referenced from the frontend.

Generating code on the level of IR is all well and good,
but we also need to be able to generate code that can then be used in the fronted,
like functions that can be called by an identifier, enumerations and variables.

* We do also want to be able to add meta-code in the middle of a procedure.

Something like:

switch type {
    ${
        for type in types {
            name := type.name;
            *{
                case types.$(types.value): {
                    print("{$name}\n");
                }
            *}
        }
    $}
}

* Instead of macros we should have generating functions,
  but then we need to differentiate between which function is a generator and which not,
  so that when we call it we call it during parsing and vice versa.
  - But the generator may also behave as a function...
  - This means that the generator needs a function signature as well? For the return type?
    - No, the compiler, thus the type checker as well, isn't executing whilst we are just generating code.
    - This will be checked during compilation and we can if we want store the stack trace as metadata in the instruction stream.
  - I think that some sort of meta checker for the generators and type agnostic procedures can be implemented on top not within.

proc normal_func(i: int, d: float) -> float
{
    return float(3 * i) / d;
}

proc another_func(l: float, a: float) -> (x: float, y: float)
{
    return (cos(a) * l, sin(a) * l);
